      {
        "id": 21516,
        "name": "Agent Bon",
        "username": "Agent_Bon",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/a/13edae/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2012-11-19T10:19:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>My gaming group and I have beaten the original Baron Blade, both Omnitrons, Dawn, Voss, Plague Rat, The Matriach, and Ambuscade on full advanced.&nbsp; We've beaten Spite on just his front advanced (little change on our normal stategy), and The Operative's front on avanced.&nbsp; We Haven't tried Spite or any of the Infernal Relics villains or Bomber Blade on full advanced yet.</p><p>I have yet to find a consistent way to beat The Organization on full advanced.&nbsp; That deck H scales strangely, a 5 hero team might actually be the worst for that.&nbsp; I'm not sure 3 would have enough resources at the right times, but things like End of Days and Rampage help get around the limits.&nbsp; I'm still thinking a team of 4 <em>might</em> have the best chance, and probably either needs Haka to Savage Mana some foes, preferably the Underbosses, or have Visionary put all the thugs back into the Villain deck.&nbsp; Inconsistently, you could do the Tempest with 2 Reclaim from the Deeps + someone preventing the villain deck from playing cards combo (or preventing damage could work too).</p><p>I think I would prefer that normal be for either casual play or allowing players to use an arbitrary hero team, while advanced be for more strategic play and care with team building.&nbsp; However, I feel that the usage of advanced has been inconsistent.&nbsp; Plague Rat's advanced changes the tactics somewhat, given the influences over infected heroes, while there was little like that in the core set.&nbsp; Spite's front advanced doesn't do much if you were planning to just not attack on the front anyway.&nbsp; The Organization sometimes beats my group without advanced at all even with a team built specifically for the deck, making advanced seem unnecesary most of the time for it (though The Chairman flipping early is tactically interesting).&nbsp; Original Baron Blade's back side advanced isn't very important because if you can beat the front on advanced then the back probably doesn't stand a chance.&nbsp; I'm not saying straight damage boosts or reductions are bad for advanced, because I find Omnitron's encourages you to play differently, and the villains should probably be overall more difficult on advanced.</p><p>Overall though, advanced is a great idea.&nbsp; When I first saw advanced on a villain card then I knew what the intention was and immediately had more respect for GtG.&nbsp; It adds quality replay value to many of the decks.</p><p>On a side-note, I'm very much against extra shuffling on advanced or otherwise because extra shuffling often delays the game and puts extra wear on the cards.</p>",
        "post_number": 29,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T01:55:36.069Z",
